# Intro
Compares the H2 abundance of two runs of the same box: one with the H2 line absorption of the
light transport switched on (`GEARFeedback:ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1`) and one with it off. One
29.7 Msun star sits in a uniform, periodic box of 30 pc side, filled with gas of
hydrogen density 30 cm^-3 and 0.08 solar metallicity, so that H2 forms on dust. The star's
Lyman-Werner (LW) light dissociates the H2. The run is cooling with geajay only, with
`h2_shielding_length_method: Jeans`; official Grackle refuses `ISRF_H2_line_absorption: 1`.

With the switch on, the star's dissociation rate is multiplied by the line transmission
`T_line` of the path (written as `LWLineTransmissions`). With the switch off, the library
multiplies it by its own H2 self-shielding factor at the Jeans length of the particle. In
photodissociation equilibrium the formation rate over the star rate equals `x T_line` with the
switch on and `x S(x n_H L_J)` with it off, where `x = n_H2 / n_H`, `S` is the shielding
function of Wolcott-Green and Haiman (2019) and `L_J` the Jeans length. The two legs hold
different gas, so the formation rate per H atom (the sum of the library channels: dust, H-,
H2+, three-body) and the star rate (proportional to `rho u_LW`) of each leg enter with that leg's
own species, density and temperature. The channels come from the library, so a PASS tests the
transmission switch, not the formation channels.
The check solves the off-leg equation for `x` of every gas particle from the on-leg state and
compares the median of `ln(x_on / x_off)` per density bin with the measured one.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=geajay_3 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-geajay=path/to/geajay --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix
make -j
```
The geajay build must provide the option that leaves the transported LW rate out of its H2
shielding. The packet transport needs a radiation table with the `Data/Radiation` group
(see the README of `ISRFH2Photodissociation`). Parameters are in `params.yml`; the
resolution, density and particle mass are set by the `level`, `gas_density`, `gas_mass`
and `h2_ratio` variables of `run.sh`.

# Run
Three runs of the same box are needed. `run.sh` runs them with the accurate (BDF) solver of
geajay (`solver=1`, the default of the script). The cheap solver holds the self-shielding
factor of the switch-off leg fixed over each sub-step, so that leg lags its equilibrium while
the H2 falls; the check reads the solver from the snapshots and reports cheap-solver legs
as INDETERMINATE, with the reason, instead of PASS or FAIL. The `repeat` run repeats the `on` run unchanged; it
measures how much the H2 differs between two runs with the same switch.
```
leg=on ./run.sh
leg=off ./run.sh
leg=repeat ./run.sh
python3 check_h2_cooling_onoff.py --on on/snap --off off/snap --repeat repeat/snap
```
The check reads snapshots 3 to 6 by default (`--first`, `--last`).

# Checking
`check_h2_cooling_onoff.py` needs the three snapshot directories, the snapshot before the
first one checked, and the `timesteps.txt` next to each snapshot directory (`run.sh` copies it).

- Each particle's root search stops at `n_H2 = n_H / 2`. A particle is left out only if it
  has no light, no formation (zero rate in a leg), no root, more than one root in that range,
  or is not in photodissociation equilibrium. The counts are printed by reason.
- The discrepancy of a density bin is the median over the bin of the per-particle residual,
  the measured minus the derived `ln(x_on / x_off)`. The bar is twice its bootstrap standard
  error (particles resampled together, the legs share them), plus the noise, plus the lag.
- The noise of a bin is the absolute median of `ln(x_on / x_repeat)` plus twice its bootstrap
  standard error.
- The lag is the median shift of the derived value when both legs use the snapshot temperature
  plus the change of one step. The cooling solve starts from the energy at the end of the step,
  so the library temperature leads the snapshot temperature; the size of one full step is an
  assumed upper bound. The change per step is that between the two snapshots divided by the
  number of steps.
- A bin is decided only with at least 30 particles and a bar of at most ln 2. A decided bin
  passes if the discrepancy is below the bar. Any other bin is printed as INDETERMINATE.
- A non-finite input of any particle (also in the previous snapshot), or a negative or
  non-finite formation rate, is counted and fails the snapshot.

A PASS means that the change of the H2 abundance between the two runs equals the change
that the written line transmission predicts, with the library formation rates, within the
bar. It does not test the transmission itself, the formation rates or the shielding fit of the
library. It cannot see an error in the line transmission or in the column smaller than the
bar (on the uniform box the lag is 90 to 99 per cent of the bar, on the collapsing box the run-to-run noise is 67 to 99 per cent), a switch fault that lowers the LW energy together with the dissociation rate, or an
error in the absolute size or sign of a formation channel.

Exit status: 0 every snapshot passes, 1 a failure (or every snapshot is not evaluated),
2 otherwise (at least one snapshot is inconclusive or has no decided bin).
`--x-on-scale`, `--t-line-power`, `--column-scale`, `--plant-nan-field`, `--plant-hm-sign` and
`--synthetic` plant a known error or replace the off leg by the solver, to confirm that the
check fails when it should. `check_official_grackle_refusal.sh` checks that official Grackle
refuses the switch.
